--- body ---

## Context

Yawn's probe (`scripts/project-state.py --tests`, `test_state()`) runs the
project's `make check-fast|check|test` target in the project's primary
checkout. Yawn's SKILL.md calls itself read-only, and that stays the contract.

Observed 2026-11-04 in VN-Power-Scenario (2018 research repo, not
harness-shaped): `test: cleaner`, `cleaner: clean`, and `clean` does `rm -f`
on every generated table and figure. A plain `/yawn` deleted 19 untracked
build outputs in the author's checkout. Regenerable with `make all` once the
environment worked; any copy differing from current code was lost. The same
Makefile's `%.txt` rule also runs `sed -i` on tracked `init.py`.

## Decision (author, 2026-11-04)

Fix at the source, not with a guard on yawn. Five parts:

1. **Yawn stays read-only; the documentation says how.** Yawn's test
   check runs the project's test target only on a clean checkout
   (`git status --porcelain` empty: no tracked change, no untracked file);
   otherwise it reports `skip: dirty tree` and runs nothing. SKILL.md states
   this and the contract it relies on (part 2). This is a precondition of
   the check, not a guard: it blocks nothing else.
2. **Injunction.** A project-facing rule: on a clean checkout, a project's
   test target (`check-fast`, `check`, `test`) is read-only: it leaves
   `git status --porcelain` empty and rewrites no tracked file.
3. **No guard.** `project-state.py` keeps running the target in place; no
   recipe parsing, no blocking check (rules/workflow.md: prefer deleting a
   guard to growing one; test-doctor owns test-hygiene diagnostics).
4. **test-doctor probe.** Occasionally, per project: make a throwaway
   detached worktree of HEAD (clean by construction), snapshot
   (`git status --porcelain` and tracked-file hashes), run the test
   target, compare. Any change breaks the part 2 contract. A diff is a diagnostic finding (report, and a ticket in
   that project's store when it has one), never a block. The worktree cost is
   paid at doctor cadence, not on every yawn.
5. **Sweep.** Run the probe once across the author's projects now; each
   offender gets a ticket in its own store (cross-project rule: ticket there,
   never edit it from here). Projects without a store are reported to the
   author.

Rejected: running yawn's tests in a throwaway worktree every time (env setup
cost on every yawn, e.g. a full uv sync); test only declared targets (loses
the signal on legacy repos); `make -n` keyword heuristic (misses other
destruction shapes).

Residual risk accepted: an unswept or new project can still be hit between
probe runs; typical damage is regenerable outputs.

## First sweep case

VN-Power-Scenario. The 2026-11-04 incident happened on a dirty checkout
(19 untracked outputs); part 1's clean-tree precondition alone would have
prevented it. On a clean checkout its `make test` (`cleaner` prerequisite)
deletes nothing tracked but removes ignored caches; the `%.txt` rule's
`sed -i ... init.py` is a no-op since VERBOSE is False on master. The probe
decides whether it meets the contract; report to the author (no ticket
store there).

## Exit criteria

- [x] `project-state.py --tests` runs the test target only when
      `git status --porcelain` is empty, else reports `skip: dirty tree`;
      test: a fixture with an untracked file and a deleting `test` target
      keeps the file after the probe.
- [x] Yawn's SKILL.md keeps "read-only" and states the clean-tree
      precondition and the project contract it relies on.
- [x] The test-target injunction is in the rule set where project-facing
      rules live, one sentence, with its reason.
- [x] test-doctor carries the throwaway-worktree probe, with a test: a
      fixture repo whose `test` target deletes an untracked file is flagged;
      a clean target is not; the primary checkout is untouched by the probe.
- [x] The probe never blocks a merge or a yawn (diagnostic only).
- [x] Sweep run across the author's projects; offenders ticketed in their
      own stores or listed for the author; VN-Power-Scenario reported.

## Decomposition (author-approved raid, 2026-11-07)

- 1462 implements the clean-yawn precondition, test-target contract, isolated doctor probe, and controls.
- 1463 performs the one-time project sweep after 1462 lands, with explicit inventory and environment limits.
- Parent closes after both children and integration review; old exit criteria are allocated between the children, not discarded.

## Integration review (2026-11-07)

Children 1462 (PR 2243, merge b61b537fce309c11e50d132782d7e4c71e767b42) and 1463 (PR 2252, merge 2fed6f71c6a0aa1d4bd3fe2dc4f9bb6329528811) were re-read together. The full declared after-merge set physically passed on 2fed6f71c6a0aa1d4bd3fe2dc4f9bb6329528811 (record time 2026-11-07T21:27:36Z), including pytest-guard and lint; no duplicate full run was commissioned.

- The common `test_state` precondition checks fresh porcelain before Make, pytest, or npm selection. `test_dirty_tree_never_runs_deleting_target`, the runner parametrizations, and unreadable-status controls preserve the untracked sentinel and skip execution.
- Yawn remains read-only. Its documentation and the single project-facing sentence in `rules/make.md` state the clean-tree contract and reason.
- The on-demand stdlib probe pins HEAD, separates execution from endpoint evidence, and retains unavailable/finding residue. Its seeded-deletion control is explicitly not a production clean-baseline claim; FIFO, timeout, missing-prerequisite, and primary-independence controls remain covered.
- The repo-wide ref union search found the probe invocation only in the named test-doctor section, with no healthcheck/schedule/gate wiring. The child report adds data and coverage only.
- The finite manifest contains eleven rows: four passing executions, two failed/inconclusive executions with unchanged endpoints, and five unprobed rows. No confirmed endpoint mutation required an offender ticket. VN-Power-Scenario is explicitly unavailable/unprobed at its known local path; it is not marked PASS, and its present target/store remain unverified.
- Unsafe setup paths and missing projects remain exclusions, not findings against their normal workflows. Exact coordinates and raw evidence remain local; public metadata is relative. Successful patient owners cleaned their outer trees; the two inconclusive nested probes and their containing patient executors remain protected.

These limits satisfy the author-approved bounded sweep allocation; no source fixes, recurring sweep, or broader discovery is inferred.
